:0.58,"def_epa_per_play":-0.02075},{"week":15,"opponent":"NYG","home":false,"pf":20.0,"pa":36.0,"off_snaps":69,"epa_per_play":-0.06956,"pass_rate":0.6377,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6383,"def_epa_per_play":0.06365},{"week":16,"opponent":"CIN","home":true,"pf":38.0,"pa":35.0,"off_snaps":84,"epa_per_play":0.1795,"pass_rate":0.6429,"pass_rate_neutral":0.8077,"def_epa_per_play":0.0558},{"week":17,"opponent":"NE","home":false,"pf":27.0,"pa":24.0,"off_snaps":68,"epa_per_play":0.1397,"pass_rate":0.6765,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6182,"def_epa_per_play":0.0366}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2025,"team":"KC","distance":0.3401,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Andy Reid"},{"season":2021,"team":"LAC","distance":0.4684,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Joe Lombardi"},{"season":2024,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.4737,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"GB","distance":0.4744,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Matt LaFleur"},{"season":2022,"team":"LAC","distance":0.4962,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2019,"team":"DET","distance":0.8349,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"LA","distance":0.8887,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Raheem Morris"},{"season":2018,"team":"GB","distance":0.8944,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"NE","distance":0.9117,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"NE","distance":0.9372,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Dolphins went 5-11 in 2019 and were outscored 494 to 306. Neither side held up, as the offense ranked 28th in EPA per play and the defense 32nd in EPA allowed. What set them apart was the 2nd highest pass rate over expected and the lowest two-high safety rate in the league.","offense":"The Dolphins offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in four of the five main efficiency measures. They threw more than almost anyone relative to the situation, even when the score did not call for it. Ryan Fitzpatrick ran the passing game, with DeVante Parker leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Dolphins had one of the worst defenses in the league, ranking 25th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. Taco Charlton led the team with 5 sacks, and Samuel Eguavoen added 3.5 while playing only 55% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the lowest two-high safety rate in the league.","special":"The Dolphins were one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 44% of their short-yardage chances in normal situations. Their kickers made 77% of field goals, which was well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the secondary, about 39% of the money on the books.","players":"Ryan Fitzpatrick played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,529 yards. DeVante Parker was the clear top target with 22% of the looks and 1,202 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Kalen Ballage and Patrick Laird. On defense, Taco Charlton led the team with 5 sacks and Nik Needham picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2025 Chiefs under Andy Reid and the 2021 Chargers under Joe Lombardi. Their defense looked most like the 2019 Lions and the 2023 Rams under Raheem Morris.","trends":"The offense went from 28th in EPA per play in 2018 to 28th in 2019, about where it was. The defense moved from 24th in EPA allowed per play in 2018 to 32nd in 2019, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was number of defenders in the box, which went from 6.53 to 5.98.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2019. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}